<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_DATA_SYNC" />
    <!-- Held by sshj transport during long reads — see PERMISSION_DECLARATIONS.md -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.WAKE_LOCK" />
    <!-- Lets the user opt the app out of OEM battery optimisation so the
         foreground service + SSH sockets aren't killed when they switch
         to a music app for a moment. Triggers the standard system dialog;
         does NOT auto-grant. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.REQUEST_IGNORE_BATTERY_OPTIMIZATIONS" />

    <!-- Haptic feedback during the FIDO touch dialog: ramp-up vibration
         while the user must keep holding the security key against the
         phone, plus a single firm pulse synchronised with the success
         checkmark animation. Without this permission `Vibrator.vibrate`
         silently no-ops and the user can't tell whether to hold or
         release. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.VIBRATE" />

    <!-- Hardware security keys.
         NFC: read FIDO2 authenticators tapped on the back of the phone
              (YubiKey 5 NFC, Nitrokey 3 NFC, etc).
         USB host: drive USB-C / USB-OTG security keys plugged into the phone.
         Both surfaces are optional — `required="false"` means devices
         without NFC or without USB-host can still install the app and
         use software keys. -->
    <!--
        ⛔ NO MEDIA-READ PERMISSIONS. DO NOT ADD THEM BACK.

        READ_MEDIA_IMAGES / READ_MEDIA_VIDEO / READ_MEDIA_VISUAL_USER_SELECTED
        were declared on 2026-07-29 to draw our own recent-photos grid inside the
        attachment sheet. Google Play REJECTED the app for it on 2026-07-30 under
        the Photo and Video Permissions policy, with enforcement applied — the
        release was blocked and production stayed on the previous version.

        The justification in this comment was factually wrong, which is worth
        recording so nobody repeats it: it claimed "the system picker cannot host
        the camera cell or live inside our UI". The EMBEDDED photo picker does
        exactly that — it renders inside the app's own layout on a SurfaceView,
        needs no permission at all, and is what Google's rejection points to
        (androidx.photopicker:photopicker-compose, Android 14 + SDK Extensions 15).

        The policy is narrow and we do not clear it: these permissions are for
        apps whose CORE function a picker cannot serve (a gallery, a photo
        editor). Ours is a convenience grid in a composer. So the sheet uses the
        system picker, which needs nothing, and READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE is gone with
        them — nothing reads MediaStore any more.
    -->
    <!--
        LIVE VIEWFINDER in the attachment sheet's first cell (Telegram-style).

        ⚠ This CHANGES the camera capture contract. Without this permission,
        ACTION_IMAGE_CAPTURE is serviced by another app under ITS permission and
        we need none. DECLARING it means Android now requires the user to grant
        it to US before that intent works too. We take that on because a live
        preview cannot be rendered any other way — the frames have to come from
        our own process.

        Not required=true: a device without a camera still installs and simply
        gets no viewfinder cell.
    -->
    <!--
        MICROPHONE. Two uses, both explicitly started:
          - a voice message the user records in the composer;
          - the bridge `audio` verb, which the server-side agent can call — and
            which is OFF BY DEFAULT behind its own switch, because it records the
            room rather than the screen (see AppPreferences.bridgeAudioAllowed).
        Never recorded speculatively, never left open in the background.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECORD_AUDIO" />
    <uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.microphone" android:required="false" />

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.CAMERA" />
    <uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.camera" android:required="false" />
    <uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.camera.autofocus" android:required="false" />


    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.NFC" />
    <uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.nfc" android:required="false" />
    <uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.usb.host" android:required="false" />

    <!--
        allowBackup: true since 0.3.9 — Google Play's "safe data transfer to a
        new device" quality requirement (2026-08). This does NOT reopen the
        SEC-7 hole (allowBackup=false, INVARIANTS.md): the rules files are
        WHITELISTS, so only the server list (Room DB) and settings
        transfer. Chat history never leaves the device, and
        passwords / private keys (encrypted_servers.xml) are excluded AND
        Keystore-bound — undecryptable anywhere else by construction, with
        SecretsStore self-healing if an OEM tool copies the file anyway.
        Cloud backup of the DB additionally requires client-side encryption;
        device-to-device transfer is a direct local channel. Details in
        res/xml/data_extraction_rules.xml.
    -->
    <application
        android:name=".ConchApp"
        android:allowBackup="true"
        android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/data_extraction_rules"
        android:fullBackupContent="@xml/backup_rules"
        android:networkSecurityConfig="@xml/network_security_config"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_round"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/Theme.Conch"
        tools:targetApi="34">

        <!--
            Multi-resume on large screens / split-screen. Android 12+ already
            opt-in via this meta-data — without it our Activity gets paused
            when the user pairs us with another app side-by-side, and our
            SSH session loses its foreground state mid-turn. Tablet & DeX
            users hit this constantly. Tied to phase 0 of the foldable
            adaptive workstream.
        -->
        <meta-data
            android:name="android.allow_multiple_resumed_activities"
            android:value="true" />

        <!--
            Tablet / foldable / DeX hint. The Play Console reads this and
            unlocks the "large screens" filter in the catalogue; otherwise
            our app gets surfaced as "phone-only" even though it adapts.
            Wire alongside the adaptive scaffolds (phase 1+).
        -->
        <meta-data
            android:name="android.max_aspect"
            android:value="100" />

        <!--
            ⚠ launchMode MUST stay singleTask. It was `singleTop`, and singleTop
            only de-dupes an activity that is already on TOP OF THE TARGET TASK —
            it does not stop a SECOND TASK being created for the same activity.
            With auto-PiP (onUserLeaveHint below) that happens constantly: the
            activity in Picture-in-Picture owns a task in the PINNED windowing
            mode, which the system cannot reuse for a fullscreen launch, so the
            next launcher tap builds a NEW task with a NEW MainActivity while the
            old one stays behind. Each round-trip left another card in recents —
            the user counted EIGHT Conch cards, every one showing a different
            chat, i.e. eight independent nav stacks and eight sets of ViewModels
            racing over the same app-scoped AgentSessionManager.
            `android.allow_multiple_resumed_activities` above let them all stay
            resumed instead of pausing each other, so nothing ever cleaned them
            up.

            singleTask means the system reuses the ONE existing instance wherever
            it lives — pinned PiP task included — and delivers the intent to
            onNewIntent instead of spawning a sibling. Safe here because the app
            is single-activity: there is nothing above MainActivity in the task
            for singleTask's clear-top to destroy, and we read nothing off the
            launch Intent that a re-delivery could lose. It also makes
            MULTIPLE_TASK / NEW_DOCUMENT flags from OEM launchers and shortcuts
            inert.
        -->
        <!--
             ⛔ NO VpnService HERE, EVER AGAIN (Play enforcement, 2026-09-01).
             0.5.0 shipped a traffic-routing toggle on VpnService.setHttpProxy;
             Play answered and required the element removed from every artifact
             of every release type. The feature is gone — see INVARIANTS.md.
             Port forwarding never used VpnService and stays.
        -->

        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:launchMode="singleTask"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize"
            android:resizeableActivity="true"
            android:supportsPictureInPicture="true"
            android:configChanges="screenSize|smallestScreenSize|screenLayout|orientation|keyboardHidden|density|uiMode"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.Conch.Splash">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
            <!--
                 NO USB_DEVICE_ATTACHED intent-filter on purpose. With the
                 permissive @xml/usb_device_filter (it matched EVERY USB device),
                 Android offered Conch as a handler for ANY device plugged into
                 the phone's Type-C port — keyboards, drives, anything. A
                 FIDO-only filter can't be expressed via USB descriptor classes
                 without brand- locking to specific VID/PIDs (and the FIDO usage
                 page isn't filterable here). We don't need auto-launch at all:
                 USB is used ONLY for FIDO keys, and yubikit's
                 `startUsbDiscovery` (UsbConfiguration.handlePermissions(true),
                 see SecurityKeyManager.awaitUsb) enumerates the key + requests
                 USB permission at CONNECT time, inside the touch dialog. So
                 plugging in any other device never touches Conch.
            -->

            <!--
                Intentionally NO TECH_DISCOVERED / NDEF_DISCOVERED filters.
                yubikit's `startNfcDiscovery` uses `NfcAdapter.enableReaderMode`
                while the active dialog is open, which intercepts ALL NFC
                dispatch at runtime regardless of manifest filters. Adding a
                TECH filter here meant any NFC tap OUTSIDE an open dialog
                relaunched MainActivity (delivering an unexpected intent), which
                made the touch flow loop on NFC-keyed users — see v1.0.8
                first-test feedback. We rely entirely on reader-mode now.
            -->
        </activity>

        <!--
            The pairing code arrives through a notification's direct-reply field,
            because Android shows that code in a dialog that cancels the pairing
            the moment it is left. The shade draws over that dialog; our own
            screen cannot.

            Not exported, and not something a stray tap can trigger: it does
            nothing without six typed digits, which is exactly the bar the
            removed PiP receiver failed to clear.
        -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".adb.PairingReplyReceiver"
            android:exported="false" />

        <service
            android:name=".service.ConchService"
            android:exported="false"
            android:foregroundServiceType="dataSync" />

        <!-- Pins the process while a local-model download runs, so a 2 GB
             file finishes with the app pocketed. Stops itself when the last
             download ends; the progress notification is the one Android
             requires for the pin anyway. -->
        <service
            android:name=".service.LlmDownloadService"
            android:exported="false"
            android:foregroundServiceType="dataSync" />

        <!--
            Hands the camera app a writable content:// URI for one photo.

            This FileProvider predates the in-sheet viewfinder and still earns
            its keep: it backs the ACTION_IMAGE_CAPTURE fallback used when the
            user has not granted CAMERA, or when the device has no viewfinder to
            offer. In that path the shot is taken by ANOTHER app under ITS own
            permission and handed back through this URI.

            CAMERA itself is declared above, for the live preview only — frames
            for a viewfinder have to come from our own process. Note the
            side effect that buys: once CAMERA is declared, the capture INTENT
            needs the grant too, which is why the fallback above is gated on it.

            `authorities` derives from ${applicationId}, so the debug build
            (applicationIdSuffix ".debug") gets its own authority and can be
            installed alongside the release one.

            exported=false + grantUriPermissions: only the camera activity we
            explicitly hand the URI to can write it, and only for that call.
        -->
        <provider
            android:name="androidx.core.content.FileProvider"
            android:authorities="${applicationId}.fileprovider"
            android:exported="false"
            android:grantUriPermissions="true">
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.support.FILE_PROVIDER_PATHS"
                android:resource="@xml/file_paths" />
        </provider>

        <!-- The PiP header's "Stop turn" action and its receiver are gone on
             purpose (2026-08-18): a RemoteAction fires with no confirmation, and
             it sat under the same tap that expands the window, so a reflex
             mis-tap killed a running turn. Stop lives in the prompt bar, where
             pressing it is a decision. Do not re-add a receiver here without a
             control that cannot be hit by accident. -->

    </application>
</manifest>
